    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> and their allies fought the segregation and disenfranchisement imposed on the South after Reconstruction with lawsuits, boycotts, sit-ins, freedom rides, marches and voter drives.</li><li>Non-violent campaigns from Montgomery to Selma met violent resistance, and the images of that violence helped persuade Congress to pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</li><li>From 1965 the movement divided over Black Power, northern poverty and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, and the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> in 1968 is often taken as the end of its classic phase.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a> was not the first to refuse: in March 1955, nine months before her arrest, the fifteen-year-old Claudette Colvin was arrested for keeping her seat on a Montgomery bus, and she was a plaintiff in the case that desegregated them.</p></aside>
    </section>

