    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1954</span><span class="rf-text">The Supreme Court rules segregated public schools unconstitutional in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1955–1956</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a>’s arrest starts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1957</span><span class="rf-text">Federal troops escort nine Black students into Central High School in Little Rock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1960–1961</span><span class="rf-text">Student sit-ins spread across the South, and Freedom Riders challenge segregated bus travel</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1963</span><span class="rf-text">The Birmingham campaign and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a>, where King speaks of his dream</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1964</span><span class="rf-text">The Civil Rights Act outlaws segregation in public places and discrimination in employment</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery marches</a> lead to the Voting Rights Act</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1968</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> is assassinated in Memphis, and Congress passes the Fair Housing Act</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws.jpg" alt="Jim Crow laws" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:White_and_colored_water_fountain_then_and_now_-_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Wilson, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteenth-amendment">Thirteenth Amendment</a> in 1865 was followed by the Fourteenth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifteenth-amendment">Fifteenth Amendments</a>, which promised citizenship, the equal protection of the laws and the vote regardless of race. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconstruction-era">Reconstruction era</a> Black men voted and held office across the South, but after federal troops withdrew in 1877 the southern states built a new order of white supremacy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a> segregated schools, trains, streetcars, parks and hospitals, and in Plessy v. Ferguson (1896) the Supreme Court accepted “separate but equal” facilities. Poll taxes, literacy tests, all-white primaries and intimidation removed most Black voters from the rolls, and lynching enforced the system: the Tuskegee Institute recorded more than 3,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> lynched between 1882 and 1968.</p><p>Resistance was never absent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People</a> (NAACP), founded in 1909, fought lynching and segregation in the press and the courts, and from the 1930s its lawyers, led by Charles Hamilton Houston and his former student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgood-marshall">Thurgood Marshall</a>, attacked segregated education case by case; in Smith v. Allwright (1944) the Supreme Court outlawed the all-white primary. The Great Migration took millions of Black southerners to the cities of the North and West, where they could vote but met segregated housing and closed trades. In 1941 A. Philip Randolph, leader of the Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters, threatened a march on Washington and won an order from President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> banning racial discrimination in defence industries.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in which more than a million African Americans served in segregated forces, sharpened the contrast between a war against racist regimes abroad and segregation at home, and Black newspapers campaigned for a “Double V”, victory over fascism and over racism. In 1948 President Harry S. Truman ordered the desegregation of the armed forces, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> made segregation an embarrassment for a country seeking allies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. On 17 May 1954, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education of Topeka</a>, the Supreme Court under Chief Justice Earl Warren ruled unanimously that “separate educational facilities are inherently unequal”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr.jpg" alt="Martin Luther King Jr." loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Lyndon_Johnson_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yoichi Okamoto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement’s central aim was to end the legal system of segregation and disenfranchisement in the South and to make real the equal citizenship promised by the Constitution. Its demands were concrete: the right to attend equal schools, an end to segregated buses, lunch counters, parks and waiting rooms, the right to register and vote without tests or terror, protection by the police rather than from them, fair employment and, increasingly, decent housing and wages. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> for Jobs and Freedom in 1963 joined these in its call for a strong civil rights law, a federal jobs programme and a higher minimum wage.</p><p>Most of its leaders shared a faith in non-violent direct action, drawn from the Black church and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>’s satyagraha: by accepting arrest and violence without striking back, protesters would expose injustice and appeal to the conscience of the nation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> spoke of a “beloved community” of the reconciled, and in his “Letter from Birmingham Jail” (1963) he defended breaking unjust laws openly and accepting the penalty, and rebuked white moderates who preferred order to justice. At the March on Washington he spoke of his dream that his children would one day be judged not by the colour of their skin but by the content of their character.</p><p>Not all agreed. Robert F. Williams in North Carolina and later the Deacons for Defense and Justice armed themselves against the Ku Klux Klan, and many activists saw non-violence as a tactic rather than a creed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-x">Malcolm X</a>, the best-known minister of the Nation of Islam until 1964, rejected integration into a white society and urged Black pride and self-reliance, and from 1966 the call for “Black Power” asked for Black-led organisations, political power and community control. Most white southern politicians defended segregation as local custom and the right of each state to manage its own affairs: in the Southern Manifesto of 1956, 101 members of Congress condemned Brown as “a clear abuse of judicial power”, and George Wallace, inaugurated as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> in 1963, promised “segregation now, segregation tomorrow, segregation forever”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott.jpg" alt="Montgomery bus boycott" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Parks_1956.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UPI, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In August 1955 Emmett Till, a fourteen-year-old from Chicago, was murdered in Mississippi after being accused of flirting with a white woman; his open-casket funeral and his killers’ acquittal by an all-white jury shocked the nation. On 1 December 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a> was arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama">Montgomery, Alabama</a>, for refusing to give up her bus seat to a white passenger. Black residents, three-quarters of the riders, boycotted the buses for 381 days, until the Supreme Court’s ruling in Browder v. Gayle desegregated them in December 1956. In 1957 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower">Dwight D. Eisenhower</a> sent paratroopers to escort nine Black students into Central High School in Little Rock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a>, after Governor Orval Faubus had used the National Guard to bar them.</p><p>On 1 February 1960 four Black students sat down at the whites-only lunch counter of a Woolworth’s store in Greensboro, North Carolina, and asked to be served. Sit-ins spread across the South, and in April student leaders founded the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee (SNCC). In May 1961 Black and white Freedom Riders rode interstate buses into the Deep South to test a ruling against segregated terminals; a bus was burned near Anniston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, riders were beaten in Birmingham and Montgomery, and more than 300 were jailed in Mississippi before the terminals were ordered desegregated. In 1962 a riot against James Meredith’s admission to the University of Mississippi left two people dead.</p><p>In 1963 King’s Southern Christian Leadership Conference (SCLC) took on Birmingham, Alabama. When the public safety commissioner, Eugene “Bull” Connor, turned police dogs and fire hoses on marching schoolchildren, the pictures went round the world, and the city’s businessmen agreed to desegregate. On 11 June President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> called for a civil rights law; hours later Medgar Evers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>’s field secretary in Mississippi, was shot dead outside his home. On 28 August some 250,000 people joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a>, and on 15 September a Klan bomb at the Sixteenth Street Baptist Church in Birmingham killed four girls.</p><p>After Kennedy’s assassination President Lyndon B. Johnson drove the bill past a Senate filibuster of more than two months and signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> on 2 July. That summer about a thousand volunteers, most of them white students from the North and West, joined Freedom Summer in Mississippi, where Klansmen murdered three civil rights workers, James Chaney, Andrew Goodman and Michael Schwerner. In Selma, Alabama, on 7 March 1965, “Bloody Sunday”, state troopers and a sheriff’s posse beat and tear-gassed voting rights marchers at the Edmund Pettus Bridge. The march to Montgomery was completed under federal protection on 25 March, and on 6 August Johnson signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rosa-parks.jpg" alt="Rosa Parks" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rosa_Parks,_November_1956_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> (1929–1968), a Baptist minister with a doctorate from Boston University, became the movement’s best-known voice as spokesman of the Montgomery boycott and president of the SCLC, founded in 1957; he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1964. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-parks">Rosa Parks</a> (1913–2005), a seamstress and long-serving secretary of the Montgomery branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>, had attended a workshop on desegregation at the Highlander Folk School a few months before her arrest, and Jo Ann Robinson’s Women’s Political Council and the union organiser E. D. Nixon turned her arrest into the boycott. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurgood-marshall">Thurgood Marshall</a> (1908–1993) argued Brown and many other cases for the NAACP and in 1967 became the first African American justice of the Supreme Court.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ella-baker">Ella Baker</a> (1903–1986), a veteran organiser for the NAACP and the SCLC, encouraged the students who founded SNCC to keep their independence and to develop leaders among ordinary people. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lewis">John Lewis</a> (1940–2020), a seminary student in Nashville, was beaten as a Freedom Rider and at Selma, chaired SNCC from 1963 to 1966 and later served in Congress for more than thirty years. Diane Nash led the Nashville sit-ins and kept the Freedom Rides going; Bob Moses led SNCC’s voter registration work in Mississippi; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fannie-lou-hamer">Fannie Lou Hamer</a> (1917–1977), a sharecropper thrown off her plantation for trying to register, was beaten in a Mississippi jail and described her ordeal before the credentials committee of the Democratic National Convention in 1964. Bayard Rustin, a pacifist who had advised King on Gandhian methods, organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a> with A. Philip Randolph.</p><p>Other leaders led older or rival organisations: Roy Wilkins the NAACP, Whitney Young the National Urban League and James Farmer the Congress of Racial Equality, which organised the Freedom Rides. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-x">Malcolm X</a> (1925–1965), who left the Nation of Islam in 1964, was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in February 1965, and Stokely Carmichael, chairman of SNCC from 1966, made “Black Power” a national slogan. White allies, from northern students and clergy to a few white southerners, took part and some died, among them James Reeb and Viola Liuzzo in the Selma campaign. Yet the movement’s strength lay above all with its ordinary participants: the domestic workers who walked to work in Montgomery, the students who filled the jails, the children who marched in Birmingham, and the sharecroppers and farmers who risked their homes, jobs and lives to register to vote.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964.jpg" alt="Civil Rights Act of 1964" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LBJ_Civil_Rights_Act_crowd.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">O. J. Rapp, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The movement’s legislative victories transformed the South. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> outlawed segregation in hotels, restaurants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and other public accommodations and discrimination in employment, and allowed federal funds to be withheld from segregated schools; the Twenty-fourth Amendment of 1964 abolished poll taxes in federal elections. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a> sent federal examiners to register voters and suspended literacy tests in the states and counties with the worst records. In Mississippi the share of Black adults registered to vote rose from under 7 per cent in 1964 to about two-thirds in 1969, and the number of Black state legislators in the eleven former Confederate states rose from 3 in 1965 to 176 in 1985. In Loving v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> (1967) the Supreme Court struck down laws against interracial marriage, and the Fair Housing Act, signed on 11 April 1968, banned discrimination in the sale and rental of housing.</p><p>The laws did not end poverty, segregation in northern cities or the anger in their poorest neighbourhoods. Five days after the Voting Rights Act was signed, rioting broke out in the Watts district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, where 34 people died; in the summer of 1967 disorders struck many cities, and 43 people died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detroit">Detroit</a> alone. The Kerner Commission warned in 1968 that the nation was “moving toward two societies, one black, one white—separate and unequal”. King’s campaign against housing segregation in Chicago in 1966 met violent white crowds and won little, and the same year SNCC turned to Black Power and soon asked its white members to leave. King also spoke out against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> in 1967 and planned a Poor People’s Campaign, while the FBI under J. Edgar Hoover, which had wiretapped him since 1963, sought to discredit him.</p><p>On 4 April 1968 King was assassinated at the Lorraine Motel in Memphis, where he had come to support striking sanitation workers; riots followed in more than a hundred cities, and James Earl Ray pleaded guilty to the killing in 1969. Many historians end the movement’s classic phase there. Its coalition had split, its southern goals had largely been written into law, and the struggle for economic equality and against segregation in practice passed to new organisations, to Black elected officials and to the courts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-washington.jpg" alt="March on Washington" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:March_on_Washington_for_Jobs_and_Freedom,_Martin_Luther_King,_Jr._and_Joachim_Prinz_1963.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Center for Jewish History, NYC, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The civil rights movement brought down the legal order of segregation built in the South after Reconstruction, and its methods and songs travelled far. In 1965 Aboriginal and white students led by Charles Perkins toured New South Wales on a “Freedom Ride” against segregation, and in 1968 and 1969 marchers of the Northern Ireland Civil Rights Association, which modelled itself on the American movement, sang “We Shall Overcome”. At home, the women’s movement, the Chicano movement, the American Indian Movement, gay liberation and the disability rights movement borrowed its language of rights and its tactics of protest and litigation.</p><p>The movement is commemorated in the federal holiday for King’s birthday, signed into law in 1983 and first observed in 1986, in the National Civil Rights Museum at the Lorraine Motel, opened in 1991, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> Memorial in Washington, dedicated in 2011. Several of its murders were tried decades later: Klansmen were convicted for the Birmingham church bombing in 1977, 2001 and 2002, Byron De La Beckwith for the killing of Medgar Evers in 1994, and Edgar Ray Killen for the Freedom Summer murders in 2005.</p><p>Historians’ views have changed. Early accounts centred on King, on national leaders and on presidents and courts, as in the biographies by David Garrow and Taylor Branch. From the 1980s studies of SNCC and of local movements, by Clayborne Carson, John Dittmer and Charles Payne among others, showed the work of local people, women and young organisers, and others recovered the tradition of armed self-defence. Jacquelyn Dowd Hall’s idea of a “long civil rights movement” (2005) traces its roots to the labour and left-wing politics of the 1930s and 1940s and extends it into the North and the 1970s, while critics argue that this blurs what was distinctive about the southern mass movement of 1954–1968. Michael Klarman has argued that Brown achieved little directly and mattered most by provoking a southern backlash whose violence turned northern opinion towards reform.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The civil rights movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at its height from 1954 to 1968, used lawsuits, boycotts, sit-ins, marches and voter registration to end the legal segregation and disenfranchisement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in the South. Its non-violent campaigns, and the violence they met, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a> and inspired movements for equal rights across the world.</em></p>

